WEBVTT

00:00:01.320 --> 00:00:03.180
SPEAKER 1: Welcome to
Beyond Golden Signals,

00:00:03.180 --> 00:00:06.960
Monitoring in the Age
of Generative AI.

00:00:06.960 --> 00:00:10.140
What makes GenAI applications
fundamentally different

00:00:10.140 --> 00:00:12.150
from the traditional software
we've been monitoring

00:00:12.150 --> 00:00:13.560
for decades?

00:00:13.560 --> 00:00:15.570
There are four key shifts.

00:00:15.570 --> 00:00:18.750
The first is
non-deterministic behavior.

00:00:18.750 --> 00:00:22.740
The second is the
variable cost structure.

00:00:22.740 --> 00:00:26.430
Third, we're facing
new attack vectors.

00:00:26.430 --> 00:00:30.460
And finally, quality
is subjective.

00:00:32.250 --> 00:00:35.430
Now that we've established why
the traditional approaches fall

00:00:35.430 --> 00:00:37.620
short and what the
new challenges are,

00:00:37.620 --> 00:00:39.510
we'll start with the
fundamental building block

00:00:39.510 --> 00:00:46.140
of all monitoring, the
golden signals, or LETS.

00:00:46.140 --> 00:00:49.770
We'll dive into the first of
the Golden Signals, latency.

00:00:49.770 --> 00:00:52.350
Latency answers that
fundamental question:

00:00:52.350 --> 00:00:54.870
How long does each request take?

00:00:54.870 --> 00:00:56.430
In traditional
monitoring, we often look

00:00:56.430 --> 00:00:58.110
at that median latency,

00:00:58.110 --> 00:01:00.660
which tells us what the
average user experiences,

00:01:00.660 --> 00:01:03.630
for example, 850 milliseconds.

00:01:03.630 --> 00:01:06.780
But we'd also look at
those extremes, the P95

00:01:06.780 --> 00:01:10.200
or P99 latencies, to
catch the slower requests

00:01:10.200 --> 00:01:13.770
and identify the true
outliers and bottlenecks.

00:01:13.770 --> 00:01:15.540
But for GenAI, you
need to track latency

00:01:15.540 --> 00:01:17.610
at a more granular level.

00:01:17.610 --> 00:01:20.610
It's not just about the total
time from the user clicking send

00:01:20.610 --> 00:01:22.410
to the response appearing.

00:01:22.410 --> 00:01:24.870
You've got to instrument the
latency at multiple stages

00:01:24.870 --> 00:01:26.910
within the GenAI pipeline.

00:01:26.910 --> 00:01:28.800
For example, the time it
takes your RAG retrieval

00:01:28.800 --> 00:01:30.780
to find relevant documents,

00:01:30.780 --> 00:01:33.600
or the time for the LLM
call itself, and finally,

00:01:33.600 --> 00:01:35.880
that total request time.

00:01:35.880 --> 00:01:38.190
Understanding the latency
breakdown is critical

00:01:38.190 --> 00:01:39.600
for optimizing and pinpointing

00:01:39.600 --> 00:01:41.910
where users are
experiencing delays.

00:01:41.910 --> 00:01:45.240
In summary, for generative
AI, track latency

00:01:45.240 --> 00:01:48.210
at multiple stages -- LLM calls,

00:01:48.210 --> 00:01:51.520
external API calls,
total request time.

00:01:53.010 --> 00:01:56.070
Now let's move on to the
second Golden Signal, errors.

00:01:56.070 --> 00:01:57.450
Errors are fundamental.

00:01:57.450 --> 00:02:00.210
We need to know what is the
error rate and what types

00:02:00.210 --> 00:02:01.530
of errors are occurring.

00:02:01.530 --> 00:02:02.940
In the world of GenAI, you need

00:02:02.940 --> 00:02:07.110
to segment your errors beyond
the classic HTTP status codes.

00:02:07.110 --> 00:02:09.900
While traditional monitoring
would cover things like 400

00:02:09.900 --> 00:02:14.280
or 500 server errors, we must
also now track a new category,

00:02:14.280 --> 00:02:16.290
that's LLM model errors.

00:02:16.290 --> 00:02:17.610
These are errors
returned directly

00:02:17.610 --> 00:02:18.720
from the model provider,

00:02:18.720 --> 00:02:21.450
like the context
length being exceeded

00:02:21.450 --> 00:02:24.900
or the model safety
filter being triggered,

00:02:24.900 --> 00:02:27.090
or the model simply
being overloaded.

00:02:27.090 --> 00:02:29.880
But the takeaway here is to
track error rates by endpoint,

00:02:29.880 --> 00:02:33.630
by model, and by user segment
to quickly identify patterns

00:02:33.630 --> 00:02:38.540
and to prevent small issues from
turning into major outages.

00:02:38.540 --> 00:02:41.090
The third signal that we
traditionally monitor

00:02:41.090 --> 00:02:42.770
is traffic.

00:02:42.770 --> 00:02:47.340
Traffic simply measures how much
demand is the system handling.

00:02:47.340 --> 00:02:50.910
We typically track this as
request per second, or RPS,

00:02:50.910 --> 00:02:53.850
over time, as you
see in this chart.

00:02:53.850 --> 00:02:55.470
Now, the goal of
traffic monitoring is

00:02:55.470 --> 00:02:57.150
to understand usage patterns.

00:02:57.150 --> 00:03:00.480
That's when your system is
busy and by how much in order

00:03:00.480 --> 00:03:04.350
to ensure that you have the
capacity to handle that load.

00:03:04.350 --> 00:03:06.930
And for GenAI
applications, this is more

00:03:06.930 --> 00:03:09.570
than just the total
number of API calls.

00:03:09.570 --> 00:03:11.760
You've got to also segment
your traffic analysis

00:03:11.760 --> 00:03:13.530
to be truly effective.

00:03:13.530 --> 00:03:15.630
This means monitoring
request patterns not just

00:03:15.630 --> 00:03:17.790
by time, but by feature.

00:03:17.790 --> 00:03:19.710
For example, is it
the internal chatbot

00:03:19.710 --> 00:03:22.890
or the external facing
summarization tool?

00:03:22.890 --> 00:03:24.210
Also the user type.

00:03:24.210 --> 00:03:29.370
Are premium users or new users
driving the most traffic?

00:03:29.370 --> 00:03:30.750
And finally, by model.

00:03:30.750 --> 00:03:32.700
Which specific model?

00:03:32.700 --> 00:03:35.430
Is it a big model, a small
model, a more expensive

00:03:35.430 --> 00:03:38.820
or cheaper model that's handling
the majority of your volume?

00:03:38.820 --> 00:03:42.030
And by segmenting this traffic,
you can optimize capacity,

00:03:42.030 --> 00:03:44.580
manage your model-specific
rate limits effectively,

00:03:44.580 --> 00:03:47.580
and ultimately
control your costs.

00:03:47.580 --> 00:03:49.710
The final signal is saturation.

00:03:49.710 --> 00:03:51.990
Saturation is all about
answering the question,

00:03:51.990 --> 00:03:54.060
how constrained are
your resources?

00:03:54.060 --> 00:03:56.160
It's generally measured by
looking at the utilization

00:03:56.160 --> 00:03:58.500
of your critical infra.

00:03:58.500 --> 00:04:02.070
However, for GenAI apps, the
bottlenecks have shifted.

00:04:02.070 --> 00:04:05.160
You must pay special
attention to two new areas.

00:04:05.160 --> 00:04:07.740
The first one is
GPU utilization.

00:04:07.740 --> 00:04:10.530
The second is API rate limits.

00:04:10.530 --> 00:04:11.820
But in the world of GenAI,

00:04:11.820 --> 00:04:13.830
your primary saturation
warnings will come

00:04:13.830 --> 00:04:16.830
from watching
infrastructure utilization

00:04:16.830 --> 00:04:20.520
and model serving capacity and
associated API rate limits.

00:04:20.520 --> 00:04:23.430
This is nothing new, but they're
new critical bottlenecks

00:04:23.430 --> 00:04:25.410
that you need to monitor
in order to ensure

00:04:25.410 --> 00:04:29.100
that you can handle
your increased traffic.

00:04:29.100 --> 00:04:30.960
All right, let's
move to the first

00:04:30.960 --> 00:04:34.440
of our three critical new
dimensions, cost monitoring.

00:04:34.440 --> 00:04:38.850
As we established, traditional
LETS metrics completely miss the

00:04:38.850 --> 00:04:41.250
financial implications of GenAI.

00:04:41.250 --> 00:04:44.250
The cost structure is highly
dynamic and unpredictable.

00:04:44.250 --> 00:04:47.370
That means if you don't have
visibility, your spending can,

00:04:47.370 --> 00:04:49.920
and often will, spiral
out of control.

00:04:49.920 --> 00:04:52.470
And in this section, we'll cover
why this is a critical problem

00:04:52.470 --> 00:04:55.020
and, more importantly,
the essential metrics

00:04:55.020 --> 00:04:58.110
and strategies you need to
implement to track your spending

00:04:58.110 --> 00:05:00.510
and maintain a healthy budget.

00:05:00.510 --> 00:05:01.320
All right, let's look

00:05:01.320 --> 00:05:03.600
at why cost monitoring
is absolutely critical,

00:05:03.600 --> 00:05:06.210
and it all boils down to
the unpredictable nature

00:05:06.210 --> 00:05:07.950
of GenAI spent.

00:05:07.950 --> 00:05:10.560
We've identified three major
ways cost can escalate

00:05:10.560 --> 00:05:12.530
without warning.

00:05:12.530 --> 00:05:15.020
The first is token creep.

00:05:15.020 --> 00:05:17.210
This happens when
maybe your engineers

00:05:17.210 --> 00:05:19.520
or product managers are
trying to improve quality,

00:05:19.520 --> 00:05:23.580
but they quietly increase the
context window for the model.

00:05:23.580 --> 00:05:26.790
Without financial review, and
because cost is directly tied

00:05:26.790 --> 00:05:31.170
to token count, this can result
in a cost increase overnight.

00:05:31.170 --> 00:05:33.270
Second, we've got model drift.

00:05:33.270 --> 00:05:35.820
This is when your team, or
myself, somebody like me,

00:05:35.820 --> 00:05:39.660
an engineer on your team, might
take a faster, cheaper model

00:05:39.660 --> 00:05:41.340
and then move into
something a little bit more

00:05:41.340 --> 00:05:43.050
but perhaps a little bit slower

00:05:43.050 --> 00:05:46.710
under the premise
of better quality.

00:05:46.710 --> 00:05:49.380
This change in model, even if
the usage volume stays the same,

00:05:49.380 --> 00:05:52.140
can escalate your spending.

00:05:52.140 --> 00:05:55.380
And finally, there
are uncached calls.

00:05:55.380 --> 00:05:58.350
In GenAI, the same query
can hit the API repeatedly

00:05:58.350 --> 00:06:01.680
if you haven't implemented
an effective caching layer.

00:06:01.680 --> 00:06:05.070
And we've seen cases where
your spend is redundant

00:06:05.070 --> 00:06:07.230
because the system is paying
for the exact same expense

00:06:07.230 --> 00:06:10.530
of model completion
over and over again.

00:06:10.530 --> 00:06:13.080
And without granular visibility
into these three areas --

00:06:13.080 --> 00:06:16.290
that's token creep, model
drift, and uncached calls --

00:06:16.290 --> 00:06:19.030
your costs can escalate rapidly.

00:06:20.160 --> 00:06:21.540
So now we know what to track,

00:06:21.540 --> 00:06:23.880
but how do we get
that granular data?

00:06:23.880 --> 00:06:26.760
The answer is a robust
cost attribution strategy.

00:06:26.760 --> 00:06:30.420
Which means one thing: You've
got to tag everything.

00:06:30.420 --> 00:06:32.760
First, feature level tagging.

00:06:32.760 --> 00:06:35.910
That means by tagging your
feature like summarization

00:06:35.910 --> 00:06:40.140
or text to speech, basically
going in and adding

00:06:40.140 --> 00:06:41.610
that feature level
tag will tell you

00:06:41.610 --> 00:06:44.460
which feature is
costing the most.

00:06:44.460 --> 00:06:45.840
Secondly, user level tagging.

00:06:45.840 --> 00:06:50.040
So using tags like user ID
or tagging by organization,

00:06:50.040 --> 00:06:52.980
it's essential for
chargeback, accurate billing,

00:06:52.980 --> 00:06:54.480
and quickly identifying patterns

00:06:54.480 --> 00:06:58.800
of abusive usage before
they drain your budget.

00:06:58.800 --> 00:07:00.930
Third, model level tagging.

00:07:00.930 --> 00:07:04.890
Tags like model or provider
give you immediate visibility

00:07:04.890 --> 00:07:08.430
into the high leverage areas
for cost optimization.

00:07:08.430 --> 00:07:10.020
You can benchmark
the true expense

00:07:10.020 --> 00:07:12.540
of using one model
versus another.

00:07:12.540 --> 00:07:15.000
Finally, endpoint level tagging.

00:07:15.000 --> 00:07:17.940
Attributing costs to a
region, environment,

00:07:17.940 --> 00:07:20.340
or provider helps
your infra planning.

00:07:20.340 --> 00:07:22.170
You can see how regional
distribution of staging

00:07:22.170 --> 00:07:25.620
versus prod can impact
your total spend.

00:07:25.620 --> 00:07:29.010
By making tagging mandatory, you
gain the necessary visibility

00:07:29.010 --> 00:07:32.670
to understand where your money
is going and more importantly,

00:07:32.670 --> 00:07:34.780
how to control your spend.

00:07:36.090 --> 00:07:37.980
All right, we're going
to move on to our second

00:07:37.980 --> 00:07:41.860
of the three new dimensions,
safety and security.

00:07:43.050 --> 00:07:47.310
Let's dive into the specifics
of the GenAI threat landscape.

00:07:47.310 --> 00:07:49.740
We've categorized them
based on their risk level,

00:07:49.740 --> 00:07:53.370
starting with the two that we
deem critical, PII leakage

00:07:53.370 --> 00:07:56.070
and data exfiltration.

00:07:56.070 --> 00:07:59.100
Next, we have two attacks
at the high risk level,

00:07:59.100 --> 00:08:02.010
prompt injection, which is
probably the most common,

00:08:02.010 --> 00:08:06.090
and the next which is
related is jailbreaking.

00:08:06.090 --> 00:08:09.120
Finally, there are two at
the medium risk level.

00:08:09.120 --> 00:08:10.530
The first is denial of wallet.

00:08:10.530 --> 00:08:12.630
That's an attack
against your budget.

00:08:12.630 --> 00:08:14.430
And the next is
model extraction.

00:08:14.430 --> 00:08:17.250
And that involves sustained
systematic probing of the model

00:08:17.250 --> 00:08:20.440
with queries to
reverse engineer it.

00:08:22.680 --> 00:08:26.100
After identifying those threats,
the next critical step is

00:08:26.100 --> 00:08:29.490
to implement the right metrics
to detect and stop them.

00:08:29.490 --> 00:08:32.100
Simply put, we need to
monitor for security breaches

00:08:32.100 --> 00:08:34.170
that don't look like errors.

00:08:34.170 --> 00:08:36.570
We focus on four
key metrics here.

00:08:36.570 --> 00:08:40.050
First, the prompt
injection rate.

00:08:40.050 --> 00:08:44.880
Second, the PII detection rate.

00:08:44.880 --> 00:08:48.300
Third, the content
moderation score.

00:08:48.300 --> 00:08:51.550
And finally, jailbreak attempts.

00:08:53.430 --> 00:08:55.680
We now arrive at arguably
the most crucial

00:08:55.680 --> 00:08:59.460
of our three new critical
dimensions, quality monitoring.

00:08:59.460 --> 00:09:02.220
As we've seen, LETS tells
you if the service is up,

00:09:02.220 --> 00:09:03.360
cost and safety will tell you

00:09:03.360 --> 00:09:05.940
if the service is budget
compliant and secure.

00:09:05.940 --> 00:09:09.090
But ultimately, for a generative
AI application, the only thing

00:09:09.090 --> 00:09:13.210
that truly matters is
whether the output is good.

00:09:14.790 --> 00:09:17.130
GenAI presents a deep
challenge when it comes

00:09:17.130 --> 00:09:18.840
to quality measurement.

00:09:18.840 --> 00:09:22.080
It's because quality in GenAI
is inherently subjective,

00:09:22.080 --> 00:09:25.980
context-dependent, and as a
result, difficult to quantify

00:09:25.980 --> 00:09:28.320
with traditional metrics.

00:09:28.320 --> 00:09:31.170
Let's look at the four core
reasons for the difficulty.

00:09:31.170 --> 00:09:34.620
First, there's no ground truth.

00:09:34.620 --> 00:09:38.430
Second, context matters.

00:09:38.430 --> 00:09:42.450
Third, hallucinations
are a major challenge.

00:09:42.450 --> 00:09:46.110
And finally, we're dealing
with subjective satisfaction.

00:09:46.110 --> 00:09:48.360
That means that we've moved
on from a binary world

00:09:48.360 --> 00:09:51.300
where it either works or
it doesn't to a spectrum.

00:09:51.300 --> 00:09:53.490
Users are judging the
response on how helpful,

00:09:53.490 --> 00:09:56.080
accurate, and complete it is.

00:09:57.540 --> 00:09:59.940
Now that we understand the
challenge of measuring quality,

00:09:59.940 --> 00:10:01.980
let's look at the essential
metrics you must implement

00:10:01.980 --> 00:10:04.860
to move beyond the simple
"it works" status codes

00:10:04.860 --> 00:10:08.250
and truly measure the
output of your GenAI app.

00:10:08.250 --> 00:10:11.670
We'd recommend tracking
these six key metrics.

00:10:11.670 --> 00:10:14.970
First, the hallucination rate.

00:10:14.970 --> 00:10:18.270
Second, the relevance score.

00:10:18.270 --> 00:10:22.080
Third, user satisfaction.

00:10:22.080 --> 00:10:25.640
Fourth, we move on to
answer completeness.

00:10:25.640 --> 00:10:28.730
And before last, we've got
retrieval augmented generating.

00:10:28.730 --> 00:10:31.490
You must get back your
retrieval quality.

00:10:31.490 --> 00:10:34.550
And finally, your
response coherence.

00:10:34.550 --> 00:10:35.910
By implementing these metrics,

00:10:35.910 --> 00:10:37.980
you gain the complete
multi-dimensional view

00:10:37.980 --> 00:10:42.310
of the quality necessary
to optimize your app.

00:10:43.620 --> 00:10:45.060
All right, so let's
bring it all together.

00:10:45.060 --> 00:10:48.360
To achieve that comprehensive
GenAI observability,

00:10:48.360 --> 00:10:52.770
we've got to move on beyond the
traditional Golden Signals.

00:10:52.770 --> 00:10:54.420
This is a complete
monitoring stack

00:10:54.420 --> 00:10:58.410
that combines the fundamental
LETS metrics, which tell you

00:10:58.410 --> 00:10:59.850
if the system is running,

00:10:59.850 --> 00:11:02.250
and then it integrates
three new dimensions

00:11:02.250 --> 00:11:03.990
that tell you how it's running.

00:11:03.990 --> 00:11:06.720
The first is cost, how
much are we spending?

00:11:06.720 --> 00:11:09.030
Second is safety or security.

00:11:09.030 --> 00:11:10.620
The third is quality.

00:11:10.620 --> 00:11:13.710
And by uniting these LETS
metrics with cost, safety,

00:11:13.710 --> 00:11:15.990
and quality, we can
gain visibility

00:11:15.990 --> 00:11:18.930
into our application's
health, financial spend,

00:11:18.930 --> 00:11:22.470
security posture, as well as
the quality of the output

00:11:22.470 --> 00:11:25.200
that your users are receiving.

00:11:25.200 --> 00:11:28.290
Now, of course, Datadog's been
working on this problem already.

00:11:28.290 --> 00:11:30.600
We cover the standard Golden
Signals and how they apply

00:11:30.600 --> 00:11:34.290
to your app, but we've
also got cost, security,

00:11:34.290 --> 00:11:37.800
and quality monitoring that will
help you monitor all of this

00:11:37.800 --> 00:11:39.930
in one central application,

00:11:39.930 --> 00:11:43.440
Datadog Large Language
Model Observability.

00:11:43.440 --> 00:11:47.130
Here's a quick summary
of LLM Observability.

00:11:47.130 --> 00:11:49.200
We've got standard metrics
that we mentioned before.

00:11:49.200 --> 00:11:51.480
So we automatically capture
and visualize key metrics

00:11:51.480 --> 00:11:54.600
like latency, errors,
tokens per second,

00:11:54.600 --> 00:11:58.590
and API rate limit usage
directly from your LLM calls.

00:11:58.590 --> 00:12:00.390
But we also give you
quality and cost.

00:12:00.390 --> 00:12:01.530
The platform allows you to track

00:12:01.530 --> 00:12:03.870
and analyze those
costs associated

00:12:03.870 --> 00:12:05.370
with different models.

00:12:05.370 --> 00:12:07.140
It also helps you
evaluate prompt

00:12:07.140 --> 00:12:10.500
and response quality via
integrated eval tools

00:12:10.500 --> 00:12:12.900
and tracing tool usage.

00:12:12.900 --> 00:12:14.280
Safety and security.

00:12:14.280 --> 00:12:17.220
Datadog provides capabilities
to monitor for security risks,

00:12:17.220 --> 00:12:19.830
including identifying and
masking PII in prompts

00:12:19.830 --> 00:12:22.980
and responses and detecting
potential prompt injections

00:12:22.980 --> 00:12:26.010
or unsafe content.

00:12:26.010 --> 00:12:27.420
And that brings us to
the end of our session.

00:12:27.420 --> 00:12:29.550
Thank you so much for your time.

00:12:29.550 --> 00:12:30.870
To recap the journey, we started

00:12:30.870 --> 00:12:32.340
by acknowledging
the Golden Signals.

00:12:32.340 --> 00:12:33.870
That's latency, errors, traffic

00:12:33.870 --> 00:12:36.780
and saturation are the
bedrock of monitoring.

00:12:36.780 --> 00:12:38.190
They're not going anywhere.

00:12:38.190 --> 00:12:41.100
But for GenAI apps, LETS
alone leaves you blind

00:12:41.100 --> 00:12:42.630
to the things that matter most.

00:12:42.630 --> 00:12:46.140
A perfect 200 OK means nothing
if your model is hallucinating,

00:12:46.140 --> 00:12:50.680
leaking PII, or burning through
your budget one token at a time.
